+# Cancellation
+
+For whoever changes pantry order cancellation or answers a member who cannot cancel. Members may cancel before
+packing starts; later requests go to the pantry volunteers. Late cancellations had left packed bags of fresh food
+unused, even after a reminder banner was introduced.
+
+## Cutoff
+
+The eligibility check is [`can_cancel(pickup_starts, now)`](../../larder/cancellation.py). It allows cancellation
+only while `now` is strictly earlier than the pickup slot's start minus two hours. At the cutoff and afterwards it
+returns `False`. For a 17:00 pickup, cancellation is allowed before 15:00 and refused at 15:00.
+
+## Why packing is the boundary
+
+Volunteers start packing two hours before each pickup slot. A cancellation before packing costs no packed bag;
+one afterwards leaves a bag unused. In October, the pantry lead reported a third consecutive Saturday with packed
+bags left on the shelf: nine that day, containing milk, bread and greens. The pantry had already thrown away
+25 bags of fresh food that month.
+
+Asking members to cancel earlier through a banner was proposed, but the banner had been present since summer and
+had not prevented the waste. The agreed response was to refuse app cancellations once packing starts. This
+history and the agreement are recorded in [issue 31](../../notes/issue-31.md).
+
+## After the cutoff
+
+The agreed fallback is for a member who cannot attend to call the pantry, where volunteers decide how to handle
+the request. The pantry lead required the pantry phone number in the refusal message so members can reach them.
+
+The repository implements the eligibility check, but contains neither a refusal-message implementation nor the
+pantry phone number. The message and contact details therefore remain unverified; the check alone does not
+demonstrate that this fallback is presented to members.
